1372:DATE_RE = re.compile(r"^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$")
1373:
1374:
1375:#  *** A PYTHON DICT LITERAL ACCEPTS A DUPLICATE KEY IN SILENCE, AND THE LAST ONE WINS.
1376:#  MEASURED ON THIS FILE, HOURS AFTER IT WAS WRITTEN. ***
1377:#
1378:#  Two rounds added `sgi_eaddr_probe.py` to MANIFEST independently -- the author of this
1379:#  checker, and the #446 round whose probe it was.  Both landed, four lines apart, and
1380:#  `len(MANIFEST)` reported ONE entry.  Nothing complained, because nothing can: by the
1381:#  time the module object exists the earlier entry is already gone.  Here the two agreed
1382:#  on CLASS, so the behaviour was identical and the only damage was a stale line.  Had
1383:#  they disagreed -- one DETECTOR, one TOOL -- the file would have carried a visible,
1384:#  reviewed, *tracked* classification that the checker silently did not use, and the
1385:#  filename tooth that refuses `*_probe.py` in class `tool` would have been reading the
1386:#  wrong entry while looking correct in the diff.
1387:#
1388:#  It has to be caught by re-reading THIS FILE'S SOURCE TEXT, because the data structure
1389:#  cannot represent the fault it suffers from.  A duplicate is a SETUP FAULT, not a
1390:#  finding: it exits before any wiring is derived, so it can never be confused with a
1391:#  probe that is genuinely unwired.
1392:#  The open() is INLINE rather than a call to read() below: this guard runs at import time,
1393:#  fifty lines before read() is defined, and the first draft died with a NameError proving it.
1394:#  It failed loudly, which is luck -- a guard placed in a branch would have failed silently.
1395:def _refuse_duplicate_keys():
1396:    with open(os.path.abspath(__file__), "r", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
1397:        src = fh.read()
1398:    #  *** AN ANCHOR THAT NO LONGER MATCHES IS ITSELF A SETUP FAULT, NOT A REASON TO SKIP,
1399:    #  AND THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS GUARD DID `continue`. ***  A flagship review MEASURED
1400:    #  the consequence: `MANIFEST =  {` -- one extra space, valid Python, invisible to a
1401:    #  reader -- makes a literal find() miss, the guard skips, and a planted duplicate key
1402:    #  runs GREEN.  That is this file's own thesis turned on itself: a fault in a guard does
1403:    #  not fail loudly, it stops guarding.  The regex tolerates whitespace, and a miss is
1404:    #  now reported rather than shrugged at.
1405:    for name in ("MANIFEST", "EXEMPT"):
1406:        m = re.search(r"^%s\s*=\s*\{" % re.escape(name), src, re.M)
1407:        if m is None:
1408:            print("PROBEWIRING_SETUP_FAULT  the %s literal could not be located in this"
1409:                  " file's own source." % name)
1410:            print("  The duplicate-key guard cannot run, and a duplicate would pass"
1411:                  " UNSEEN.  Fix the anchor, do not delete the check.")
1412:            sys.exit(2)
1413:        i = m.start()
1414:        j = src.find("\n}", i)
1415:        seen, dupes = set(), []
1416:        for k in re.findall(r'^\s*"([^"]+)"\s*:', src[i:j], re.M):
1417:            (dupes.append(k) if k in seen else None)
1418:            seen.add(k)
1419:        if dupes:
1420:            print("PROBEWIRING_SETUP_FAULT  %s names the same key twice; a dict literal" % name)
1421:            print("  keeps only the LAST and says nothing.  Delete the stale line(s):")
1422:            for k in sorted(set(dupes)):
1423:                print("    %s" % k)
1424:            sys.exit(2)
1425:
1426:
1427:_refuse_duplicate_keys()
1428:
1429:#  A python interpreter token.  Bare python/python3, or the $PY-style indirections other
1430:#  harnesses in this tree use.  Anchored so python3.12 and mypython do not match.
1431:#  *** THE TRAILING BOUNDARY IS LOAD-BEARING AND THE FIRST VERSION LACKED IT. ***  A
1432:#  flagship review MEASURED that `"$PYLINT" probe.py` scored as WIRING: the alternation
1433:#  had no boundary after the group, so ANY $PY-prefixed variable read as an interpreter
1434:#  and a linter invocation would have marked a probe wired.  That is the FALSE ACCEPT
1435:#  direction -- the one that silently stops demanding a gate.  Latent rather than live:
1436:#  no such variable exists in the battery today.  `$PYTHON` and `$PY` still match;
1437:#  `$PYLINT` and `$PYTEST` no longer do.
1438:INTERP_RE = re.compile(
1439:    r'"?\$\{?(?:PY|PY3|PYTHON|PYTHON3)\}?"?(?![A-Za-z0-9_])'
1440:    r'|(?<![\w./-])python3?(?![\w.-])'
1441:)
1442:
1443:#  COMMAND POSITION -- the whole defence against "a mention counts as wiring".  Everything
1444:#  before the interpreter must be shell scaffolding: leading space, an assignment opening a
1445:#  command substitution (out=$( ), control keywords, and environment-variable prefixes.
1446:#
1447:#  THE LINE THIS WAS BUILT AGAINST IS REAL AND LIVE, not invented:
1448:#      gate_hygiene.sh:331   note "diag_div_sweep.py or python3 missing -- class ratchet NOT run"
1449:#  It carries a probe basename AND the token python3 on one non-comment line, and it is a
1450:#  message saying the probe DID NOT RUN.  A two-sided "name near python" regex scores it as
1451:#  execution.  Here the interpreter's prefix is `note "diag_div_sweep.py or `, which is not
1452:#  scaffolding, so the line is rejected.
1453:CMDPOS_RE = re.compile(
1454:    r"""^\s*
1455:        (?: [A-Za-z_]\w* = (?:\$\()? \s* )?
1456:        (?: (?: if | then | else | elif | while | until | do | ! | \$\( | \( ) \s* )*
1457:        (?: [A-Za-z_]\w* = (?: "[^"]*" | '[^']*' | \S* ) \s+ )*
1458:        $""",
1459:    re.X,
1460:)
1461:
1462:VAR_RE = re.compile(r"^\$\{?([A-Za-z_]\w*)\}?$")
1463:ASSIGN_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*([A-Za-z_]\w*)=(\S+)")
1464:
1465:
1470:
1471:def unquote(tok):
1472:    tok = tok.strip().rstrip(");,")
1473:    if len(tok) >= 2 and tok[0] == tok[-1] and tok[0] in "\"'":
1474:        tok = tok[1:-1]
1475:    return tok.strip("\"'")
