1375:#  *** A PYTHON DICT LITERAL ACCEPTS A DUPLICATE KEY IN SILENCE, AND THE LAST ONE WINS.
1376:#  MEASURED ON THIS FILE, HOURS AFTER IT WAS WRITTEN. ***
1377:#
1378:#  Two rounds added `sgi_eaddr_probe.py` to MANIFEST independently -- the author of this
1379:#  checker, and the #446 round whose probe it was.  Both landed, four lines apart, and
1380:#  `len(MANIFEST)` reported ONE entry.  Nothing complained, because nothing can: by the
1381:#  time the module object exists the earlier entry is already gone.  Here the two agreed
1382:#  on CLASS, so the behaviour was identical and the only damage was a stale line.  Had
1383:#  they disagreed -- one DETECTOR, one TOOL -- the file would have carried a visible,
1384:#  reviewed, *tracked* classification that the checker silently did not use, and the
1385:#  filename tooth that refuses `*_probe.py` in class `tool` would have been reading the
1386:#  wrong entry while looking correct in the diff.
1387:#
1388:#  It has to be caught by re-reading THIS FILE'S SOURCE TEXT, because the data structure
1389:#  cannot represent the fault it suffers from.  A duplicate is a SETUP FAULT, not a
1390:#  finding: it exits before any wiring is derived, so it can never be confused with a
1391:#  probe that is genuinely unwired.
1392:#  The open() is INLINE rather than a call to read() below: this guard runs at import time,
1393:#  fifty lines before read() is defined, and the first draft died with a NameError proving it.
1394:#  It failed loudly, which is luck -- a guard placed in a branch would have failed silently.
1478:def gate_list(regress):
1479:    """The battery's OWN array is the authority on which scripts run.
1480:
1481:    Deriving from run.sh rather than from a gate_*.sh glob is deliberate, and carries a
1482:    second-order property worth having: a gate script that exists but was dropped out of
1483:    GATES does not run, so every probe it owned becomes unwired here and this goes RED.
1484:    A glob would have called those probes gated forever.
1485:    """
1486:    run = os.path.join(regress, "run.sh")
1487:    if not os.path.isfile(run):
1488:        return None, "regress/run.sh is missing -- cannot tell which gates run"
1489:    m = re.search(r"^GATES=\(([^)]*)\)", read(run), re.M)
1490:    if not m:
1491:        return None, "regress/run.sh has no GATES=( ... ) array -- cannot derive the battery"
1492:    return [n + ".sh" for n in m.group(1).split()], None
