==========================================================================================
#485 wdcnoirq -- RUNG-3 WITNESS, REBUILT AND PRESERVED.   2026-08-27
==========================================================================================
VERDICT:  the rung-3 witness EXISTS AGAIN AND IT MEASURES.  Same script, both builds:

              PRE-FIX (d819188^ = cc27fdf)     7 / 9      the two defect rows RED
              POST-FIX (d819188)               9 / 9      same script, all green

The round's ledger field can move from `detector:` to
`probe:_scratchpad/witness485/wdcnoirq_rung3_probe.py`.

*** THESE ARE THIS FILE'S OWN NUMBERS, NOT THE LOST SEAT'S. ***  The seat reported 2/5
pre-fix and 5/5 post-fix.  That row set no longer exists, so nothing here reproduces its
denominator and no claim is made that it was the same test.  What is reproduced is the
CONCLUSION: the two arms #485 fixed deliver no interrupt before the fix and do after it,
on unmodified `-E landisk`, through real guest instructions and real address decode.

==========================================================================================
WHAT WAS BUILT, AND WHY IT IS A SINGLE VARIABLE BY CONSTRUCTION
==========================================================================================
Both trees are `git archive d819188` extracted twice; the PRE tree then has ONE file
replaced with `git show d819188^:src/devices/dev_wdc.c`.  Building PRE as "the HEAD tree
with one file reverted" rather than as a checkout of cc27fdf is deliberate: the two
commits also differ in CHANGELOG.md, regress/ and tools/, none of which reach the
emulator -- but a whole-tree checkout makes that a claim instead of a construction.

  diff -rq PRE/src POST/src        ->  dev_wdc.c and nothing else
  grep -c 'int_assert = 1;'        ->  PRE 17,  POST 19        (the two added statements)
  md5 gxemul  PRE   b9ada1ae1ef09b97f6ed79fa1ea3dab9
  md5 gxemul  POST  9f8b2ce816528767e2e356fb32c87051
  binaries differ, and both are NEWER than their own dev_wdc.c (checked in the build
  script, because MAKE_RC has been observed reporting 0 on a failed build)

Neither tree is `C:\DocumentNoSnc\CC\GXEMUL\build\`, and nothing under GXEMUL-SEC was
written.  Build trees: /tmp/gxw485pre and /tmp/gxw485post (WSL Gentoo), both scratch.

  *** THE POST-FIX BUILD IS PINNED TO d819188, NOT TO TODAY'S HEAD, AND THAT IS CHECKED
  RATHER THAN ASSUMED. ***  HEAD moved to 6faba0f (#487) while this ran.  dev_wdc.c
  changed in 77b7c03 and 6faba0f -- COMMENTS ONLY: `gcc -fpreprocessed -E -P` gives 800
  lines from each and `cmp` reports them IDENTICAL, and `int_assert = 1;` is 19 at both
  revisions.  See check_head_drift.sh.  So the post-fix binary is behaviourally current.

==========================================================================================
THE MACHINE
==========================================================================================
  machine     -E landisk -M 64, UNMODIFIED machine description, nothing device_add'ed
  wdc         machine_landisk.c: `wdc irq=...irq[SH4_INTEVT_IRQ10] addr_mult=2
              addr=0x14000000`  ->  register N at 0x14000000 + 2N, reached through P2
              ERROR   reg 1  ->  0xb4000002
              COMMAND reg 7  ->  0xb400000e
  RAM         physical 0x0c000000; guest code at 0x8c010000, VBR 0x8c020000,
              vector 0x8c020600, scratch 0x8c011000, ERROR read-back 0x8c012000
  interrupt   IRQ10 = INTEVT 0x340 -> LEVEL 5 by sh_update_interrupt_priorities()'s
              `15 - ((intevt - IRQ0)/0x20)`; sr = 0x40000000 gives MD=1, BL=0, IMASK=0
  disk        -d iR:/tmp/wdc485scratch.img  (a copy of _images/wdc-scratch-64m.img)

  command (the exact one, from run_485_witness.sh):

    python3 -u _scratchpad/witness485/wdcnoirq_rung3_probe.py \
        /tmp/gxw485<pre|post>/gxemul \
        /mnt/c/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/_images/openbsd76-landisk-bsd.rd \
        /tmp/wdc485scratch.img <tag>

  and each row's own emulator invocation, recorded in every transcript, is

    gxemul -V -E landisk -M 64 -d iR:/tmp/wdc485scratch.img <kernel>

==========================================================================================
THE ROWS -- every value produced by a guest instruction through real address decode
==========================================================================================
                                                       PRE (cc27fdf)      POST (d819188)
  sec f5 irq        *** DEFECT ***  0xF5 -> vector      silent      FAIL   0x8c020600  ok
  sec f5 landed     stimulus proof  ERROR after store   0x04        ok     0x04        ok
  unimpl aa irq     *** DEFECT ***  0xAA -> vector      silent      FAIL   0x8c020600  ok
  unimpl aa warn    stimulus proof  fatal() count       1           ok     1           ok
  unimpl aa landed  stimulus proof  ERROR after store   0x04        ok     0x04        ok
  live ec irq       LIVENESS        0xEC -> vector      0x8c020600  ok     0x8c020600  ok
  attrib noram      ATTRIBUTION     RAM store, silent   0x000000f5/silent ok   same    ok
  devsig err        DEVICE SIG      ERROR 0x01 -> 0x04  0x01->0x04  ok     0x01->0x04  ok
  ram live          ENCODING        mov.b pair on RAM   0x5a        ok     0x5a        ok
                                                        ----------------   --------------
                                                            7 / 9              9 / 9

  THE DIFFERENTIAL -- the rows the defect owns, same script, opposite colour:

    sec f5 irq        silent  ->  0x8c020600
    unimpl aa irq     silent  ->  0x8c020600

  and nothing else moved.

==========================================================================================
THE THREE CONTROLS
==========================================================================================
LIVENESS      `live ec irq`.  0xEC WDCC_IDENTIFY already asserted before #485, so it must
              deliver on BOTH builds -- and it does, at the same vector, in the same
              script.  That is what separates "the fix works" from "this script cannot
              deliver anything", and it is the row that makes the two silences above
              meaningful.

ATTRIBUTION   `attrib noram`.  The same `mov.b r8,@r7`, the same sr/vbr/breakpoint, the
              same free-run -- with r7 pointing at RAM instead of at the command
              register.  Nothing is delivered on either build, so the deliveries above
              are not the RTC, the SCIF or a timer.  It carries its own execution proof:
              the store is STEPPED and DUMPED (0x000000f5 read back through the debugger)
              before the pc is rewound and the identical instruction is free-run, so
              "silent" here cannot mean "the guest never ran".

DEVICE SIG    `devsig err`.  A guest load of the ERROR register before and after a 0xF5
              store reads 0x01 then 0x04.  0x01 is DEVINIT's power-on `d->error = 1`;
              0x04 is WDCE_ABRT.  RAM at 0xb4000002 answers 0x00 and cannot CHANGE as a
              result of a command, so the responder is the device and not memory.
              *** READ UNCONDITIONALLY. ***  It runs in its own stepped session on both
              builds rather than inside the interrupt handler: reading it only when the
              interrupt lands would make it unmeasurable on the build where the interrupt
              does not land, and the row would then fail for two reasons at once.
              `ram live` backs it up from the other side -- the identical mov.b store/load
              pair against plain RAM returns 0x5a -- so a device row reading 0x00 could
              not have been blamed on a bad hand-assembled encoding.

==========================================================================================
"SILENT" IS A POSITIVE READING HERE, NOT AN ABSENCE
==========================================================================================
An absence proves nothing until the stimulus is known to have arrived.  Each delivery leg
is broken back into the debugger with SIGINT after its spin (emul.c installs
debugger_activate as the SIGINT handler) and the ERROR register is then read BY A GUEST
LOAD.  On the PRE-FIX build both defect legs report, in one session:

    ERROR == 0x04   the free-running store reached wdc_command() and the arm set ABRT
    no BREAKPOINT   and no interrupt was delivered

The 0xAA leg carries a second, independent proof on a different channel: the
un-silenceable `[ wdc: WARNING! Unimplemented command 0xaa ... ]` fatal() appears in the
FREE-RUNNING transcript, count exactly 1.  Two channels, one fact.

The delivery oracle is `BREAKPOINT: pc = 0x8c020600`, printed by cpu_dyntrans.c through
fatal(), which has no quiet_mode early-out -- so it is un-silenceable and it NAMES THE
ADDRESS.  The rows compare that address against the interrupt vector rather than asking
the weaker question "did the process come back to a prompt", which a hundred other things
also satisfy.

==========================================================================================
THE NEGATIVE CONTROL, AND IT IS THE TRAP THIS WITNESS MOST EASILY FALLS INTO
==========================================================================================
wdc_command() opens with a no-disk early return that ALREADY sets `d->int_assert = 1` on
both builds.  Run the identical rows with no `-d` (WDCNOIRQ_NODISK=1), on the PRE-FIX
binary:

    sec f5 irq        0x8c020600  ok        <-- GREEN ON THE UNFIXED BUILD
    unimpl aa irq     0x8c020600  ok        <-- GREEN ON THE UNFIXED BUILD
    unimpl aa warn    0           FAIL      <-- and this is the row that says why
    WDCNOIRQ_RESULT=8/9

So a witness built without a disk image would have reported the unfixed binary as fixed on
exactly the two rows the round is about, and only the fatal()-count row would have
objected.  Measured, not reasoned: rows_pre_nodisk.out.

==========================================================================================
WHAT IS PRESERVED, AND WHERE
==========================================================================================
  _scratchpad/witness485/
    wdcnoirq_rung3_probe.py        THE WITNESS.  Self-contained; takes binary, kernel,
                                   disk, tag.  Its header carries every derivation.
    run_485_witness.sh             drives both builds plus the no-disk negative control
    build_485_witness_trees.sh     builds the two single-variable trees from the tar
    check_head_drift.sh            proves d819188..HEAD is comment-only in dev_wdc.c
    tree_d819188.tar               the exact tree both builds came from.  Regenerable
                                   with `git archive d819188 -o tree_d819188.tar`
    dev_wdc_PREFIX_cc27fdf.c       the one reverted file
    dev_wdc_POSTFIX_d819188.c      the fixed file
    dev_wdc_HEAD_6faba0f.c         today's HEAD, for the drift check
    build_485_witness_trees.log    the build record (single-variable check, rc, mtimes)
    configure_*.log  make_*.log    both builds
    rows_*.out  transcript_*.log   the LIVE outputs -- a re-run OVERWRITES these

    run_20260827/                  *** THE IMMUTABLE COPY THIS FILE CITES. ***
      rows_pre.out  rows_post.out  rows_pre_nodisk.out
      transcript_pre.log  transcript_post.log  transcript_pre_nodisk.log
      run_485_witness.log
      gxemul_pre_cc27fdf           b9ada1ae1ef09b97f6ed79fa1ea3dab9
      gxemul_post_d819188          9f8b2ce816528767e2e356fb32c87051

  The stamped copy exists because the driver reuses log names on every run: a citation
  into a buffer that gets overwritten is not a citation, and this project has already had
  a round block point at a log that had come to say something else.  Both BINARIES are
  kept too, so the scores can be re-checked without a rebuild -- which is the whole point
  of a task whose subject is a measurement whose artifact did not survive.

==========================================================================================
NOT SETTLED / OUT OF SCOPE
==========================================================================================
* Whether the lost seat's 5-row set measured the same things.  Its script is gone; this
  is a rebuild of the conclusion, not of the test.
* GUEST-DRIVEN reachability.  The witness drives the arms with a hand-placed guest
  instruction, which is what rung 3 asks for.  It says nothing about how often a real
  OpenBSD boot reaches them; the boot census that #485's comment once asserted is a
  separate, still-unpreserved measurement and is not re-taken here.
* nIEN / Device Control.  Not exercised.  #485's own note (WDCTL_IDS read nowhere in
  dev_wdc.c; landisk's aux registers unmapped) is unaffected either way by these rows.
