1043:#  ---------------------------------------------------------------- #497 widthkillclass
1044:#  *** THIS PROBE IS FOR AN SGI/MIPS MACHINE AND IT IS WIRED INTO THE GATE NAMED `arm`.
1045:#  That is following a precedent, not endorsing it. ***  This gate already runs
1046:#  footbridge_sites_probe.py and pit8253_latch_probe.py -- neither of which is an ARM
1047:#  instruction test either -- so it has become the tree's general DEVICE-probe gate by
1048:#  accretion.  *** BUT THOSE TWO AT LEAST RUN THIS GATE'S OWN `-E cats` MACHINE, AND
1049:#  THIS ONE DOES NOT: it is the FIRST probe here on a foreign machine AND CPU family.
1050:#  The PIT wiring comment says outright that it was placed here BECAUSE this is the only
1051:#  gate whose machine it shares. ***  So this is not a third instance of an existing
1052:#  accretion; it is a new kind of one, and the sentence above is true while its
1053:#  implication was generous to the decision it defends.  The architecture-named alternative, gate_mips.sh, is an 82-line RIG-BOOT
1054:#  gate with no probe infrastructure at all; putting a 35-row detector there would change
1055:#  what that gate is for.
1056:#
1057:#  Recorded rather than silently compounded: the right answer is a gate_devices.sh split,
1058:#  which is a harness round of its own and owes its own controls.  FILED as
1059:#  `gatedevicesplit`, with a forcing criterion: the NEXT device probe wired into
1060:#  this gate converts it from filed to OWED.
1061:#  *** #498 sgidewidth.  WIRING THIS PROBE FIRES A PRE-SPECIFIED TRIGGER, AND THE TRIGGER
1062:#  IS HONOURED RATHER THAN QUIETLY PASSED. ***  The #497 block below filed `gatedevicesplit`
1063:#  with a forcing criterion in its own words: "the NEXT device probe wired into this gate
1064:#  converts it from filed to OWED."  This is that probe.  The row is moved to OWED in the
1065:#  same commit -- a criterion that fires and is ignored is worse than one never written,
1066:#  because the next reader trusts it.
1067:#
1068:#  It is wired HERE anyway, and deliberately: the split is a harness round owing its own
1069:#  controls, and holding a HIGH device fix hostage to a gate refactor would be the tail
1070:#  wagging the dog.  What is NOT acceptable is compounding it silently.
1071:DEWLOG=$LOGDIR/sgi_de_width.log
1072:python3 sgi_de_width_probe.py "$PMAX" gate > "$DEWLOG" 2>&1 || true
1073:
1074:#  SENTINEL FIRST -- a preserved log is verified by its completion sentinel, never by its
1271:#  loop iteration.  DE_MODE_LIN_B (0x1400) is one of the FIVE modes crmfbreg.h documents and extracts to
1272:#  tlb_mode 5, so it was reachable with a DOCUMENTED mode.  Now declined, with a latched announce.
1273:#
1274:#  The probe is BOTH the rung-3 WITNESS (7/8 red on the pre-fix build under the re-pinned oracle, preserved as
1275:#  artifacts/sgirandompixel/witness_20260906/prefix_repinned_RED.out) and the DETECTOR (kill table in
1276:#  artifacts/sgirandompixel/killtable/: five mutants, all killed, BASE 8/8 opening and closing).
1277:#
1278:#  `lin_b diag once' IS THE ROW THAT NEEDED THE MOST CARE and the reason the probe draws a 1x4 rectangle
1279:#  rather than a single pixel: with one pixel an UNLATCHED announce also fires exactly once, the count channel
1280:#  cannot vary, and M_NOLATCH escaped 8/8.  Height rather than width, because LIN_A addressing is
1281:#  `ofs = x & 4095' -- y is ignored, so four rows rewrite one byte and the VALUE oracle survives.
1282:RANDLOG=$LOGDIR/sgi_re_randompixel.log
1283:python3 sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py "$PMAX" gate > "$RANDLOG" 2>&1 || true
1284:
1285:if ! grep -q "SGIRAND_DONE" "$RANDLOG"; then
1286:    check "sgi_re randompixel probe completed" "no" "yes"
1287:else
1288:    check "sgi_re randompixel probe completed" "yes" "yes"
1289:    check "sgi_re randompixel witness verdict" "$(count "$RANDLOG" '^SGIRAND_WITNESS=PASS$')" "1"
1290:    #  TWO SPACES AFTER $v, NOT ONE -- the sibling block above uses the same idiom and this block did not,
1291:    #  which the gate caught on its first run: `lin_b diag' got=2, because `^lin_b diag .*ok$' also matches
1292:    #  the row `lin_b diag once'.  That is the padded-column trap in its MATCHES-TWO-ROWS direction.
1293:    #
1294:    #  The probe prints names with "%-16s" followed by an explicit space, so a name of N chars is followed
1295:    #  by (16-N)+1 spaces.  The longest name here is `lin_b diag once' at 15, leaving exactly two -- so this
1296:    #  pattern has ONE CHARACTER of margin.  If a name must grow past 15, widen the probe's "%-16s"
1297:    #  in the same diff.
1298:    #
1299:    #  *** CORRECTED 2026-09-07 (#505 pass 2, the kimi substitute).  This used to say a name of 16 or
1300:    #  more makes the pattern "UNSATISFIABLE, which is the same trap in its other, SILENT direction:
1301:    #  the check stops testing anything and stays green."  THE SILENT PART IS FALSE. ***  `count'
1302:    #  returns 0, `check' compares 0 against the wanted "1", and 0 != 1 FAILS -- loudly.  Measured
1303:    #  over rendered rows at lengths 15/16/17.  An unsatisfiable pattern is silent only where a check
1304:    #  wants ZERO, which no row check here does.  The dangerous direction is the ONE-SPACE form,
1305:    #  which matched `lin_b diag once' from `lin_b diag' and is what this block was written for.
1306:    for v in "live" "devsig" "copy lin_a" "lin_b not src" "lin_b is fg" "lin_b alive" "lin_b diag" "lin_b diag once"; do
1307:        check "sgi_re randompixel row: $v" "$(count "$RANDLOG" "^$v  .*ok$")" "1"
1308:    done
1309:
1310:    #  DENOMINATOR, SUMMED ARITHMETICALLY -- two rows, not one, for the sibling block's reason.
1311:    check "sgi_re randompixel rows emitted" \
1312:          "$(( $(count "$RANDLOG" " ok$") + $(count "$RANDLOG" " FAIL$") ))" "8"
1313:    check "sgi_re randompixel rows green" "$(count "$RANDLOG" " ok$")"   "8"
1314:    check "sgi_re randompixel rows red"   "$(count "$RANDLOG" " FAIL$")" "0"
1315:fi
