VERDICT: The class-field escape is real, but the packet’s census and two “settled” premises need correction: there are **25 witnesses, eight with post-fix outcomes in their notes, and a ninth confirmed by preserved results**.

Premise 1’s visibility claim is verified. An ungated detector needs an exemption; an ungated witness receives `not run (correct)`. However, the checker does **not** enforce that an exemption names an owing round: it consumes the date, while the accompanying explanation is free text. Adding an exemption to a witness currently produces `ORPHAN EXEMPTION`. [Classification and exemption handling](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:2195)

Premise 2 understates the distinction. The checker defines a detector by its **repaired-property assertion**, and a witness by its **pre-fix-symptom assertion**, which becomes red after repair. “Post-fix green was never measured” alone does not establish that something is a true witness. [Definitions](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:54)

Premise 4 is false as a universal historical claim: this checker explicitly says **“RETROACTIVE IN DOMAIN, GREEN AT INTRODUCTION”** and explains why it rejected grandfathering. The requested forward-only precedent does exist in `check_bugfile_sync.py`, with `CUTOFF = "2026-08-20"` and missing dates treated as new. I treat a cutoff and the requested controls as requirements for the proposed round. [Existing wiring policy](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:78), [dated-cutoff precedent](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/check_bugfile_sync.py:42)

